00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:27.240
And it's not a big
secret that most people,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.850
when they want to deploy some
sort of the event analytics

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.845
pipeline or platform,
they start with

00:04:32.845 --> 00:04:34.770
the off-the-shelf products.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.170
And there are so many available
solutions on the market,

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.850
products such as
Mixpanel or Segment

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:46.620
or even Google Analytics or
Kissmetrics and so many others.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.310
And don't get me wrong.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.310
All of those solutions
are excellent

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.050
and they definitely
have their own market.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.620
However, this session
will focus on how

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.880
you can build your own
event analytics platform.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.730
And why would you do
that, you may ask.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:07.050
Well, usually it's because
the off-the-shelf platforms,

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:11.400
they won't be as
flexible as you'd like

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.890
or sometimes they
have a very high cost.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:20.430
And the cost is usually
some sort of the per event

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.410
price that you pay.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.650
Usually it's gradually
decreasing over volume,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.980
like if you have more
events, you will probably

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.390
pay a little bit less.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:34.290
However, at a certain
scale, the price

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:39.450
can go up as high as tens of
thousands of dollars per month.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:43.320
And in that case, in
the Jelly Button case

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:47.820
specifically, they were spending
between $45,000 to $65,000

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:54.540
per month on their off-the-shelf
event analytics platform,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.990
depending on the how
popular their game was

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.490
on a certain month.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.280
And finally,
another problem that

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.870
usually exists with the
off-the-shelf solution,

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.160
is they don't allow
you to easily own

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.670
your own data,
meaning that making

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:14.910
it accessible and available
to your other systems

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.570
and services.

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:22.080
And for example, you can't use
your own BI tool of choice.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:26.010
And you will have to use
the charting and reporting

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.640
engine that is a part of
the off-the-shelf tool.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.630
And you can't give
your data analysts

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:38.220
an ability to query your
data using standard SQL.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.050
And usually you will
need to use some sort

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.300
of a proprietary language.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.540
And there are many
more examples why

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.790
those systems can be a little
bit limiting sometimes.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:56.730
So for a couple of
years, Jelly Button

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:59.640
were using the off-the-shelf
analytics platform to the point

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:03.380
that it wasn't longer
financially feasible

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.440
and the flexibility
wasn't good enough.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.450
So Jelly Button has decided
to build a new system,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:13.050
and the design principle
of that new system

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.690
would be it has to be
flexible in the way

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:17.520
that it will allow
them to analyze

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.460
their data in any
way they would like

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:25.230
using standard SQL or
any BI tool of choice.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:27.750
It should be cheap
to build, operate,

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.330
and maintain over time.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.160
And it should have a
much better economy

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.440
of scale comparing to the
off-the-shelf solution

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.090
that they had.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.370
And finally, it
has to be global.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:43.470
Since there are players
anywhere in the world,

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:47.661
they wanted the events to be
sent with the lowest latency

00:07:47.661 --> 00:07:48.160
possible.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:56.490
And so based on all
of those design goals,

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:00.690
we came up with the following
high-level architecture

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:03.900
of their own event
analytics pipeline.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.590
And let me walk you through this
architecture at the high level

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.925
before we are going to deep
dive into the specifics of each

00:08:10.925 --> 00:08:11.550
and every part.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.880
So first, we have
the ingestion part.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.330
This is where we get the
event from mobile devices

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.920
and process them.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:26.370
In general, there are two
distinct types of events.

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:29.400
One type are the events
being sent from the game

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.830
itself, from the mobile device.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:35.760
For example, users logging
in, going from one level

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:40.200
to the other, or
accumulating stars

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.559
or doing in-app purchases.

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:46.890
Another kind of the event
are the events generated

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.260
by the gaming backend.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.710
These are more
system-level events

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:54.960
and usually they
are good indicators

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.170
of the overall platform health.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.380
The next big part
is event processing.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:04.770
This is where we
validate the events,

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.610
parse them, store them
for future analysis.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.430
This is also where we
calculate certain metrics,

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.370
such as leaderboards, or
in reaching the events

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:16.500
with the other data, such
as geolocation of users,

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.300
for example.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:23.430
And finally, the last piece is
where we generate the reports

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:29.070
and allow the data scientists
to analyze the events.

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:34.750
So now lets take a closer look
at the event ingestion part.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:39.420
So to ingest events, we need to
have some sort of the RESTful

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.440
API with which our mobile
SDK can communicate and sense

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.000
events.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.020
And we have written an initial
version of our API, Node.js.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.380
And we have decided to run
it on top of the Kubernetes

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:58.080
or specifically, GKE, which is
a Google-managed Kubernetes.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:03.899
So you may ask why have we
choose GKE to run our API.

00:10:03.899 --> 00:10:06.190
Well, we have considered
other options, such as Compute

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:08.170
Engine, for example.

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:10.930
But we wanted
something more managed

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:15.540
so we wouldn't be bothered
with things like autoscaling

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:22.570
or monitoring or doing version
upgrades of our software.

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:25.040
We have also
considered App Engine,

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.830
which is a Google Platform
as a service, which

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:33.080
we have used many,
many times in the past

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.910
for our other solutions.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:39.380
However, App
Engine, as of today,

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.960
while it has some
really beautiful tools

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:44.380
for seamless deployments
and autoscaling,

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:49.580
it is a little bit limited to
only one geographical region.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:54.220
And we wanted to have multiple
endpoints in many places

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.930
and in multiple geographies.

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:59.990
So finally, the
reason that we have

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:03.140
used GKE is because GKE
have gave us exactly the

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:04.460
features we needed--

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:07.050
fully managed
infrastructure on one hand,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:09.440
but with an ability to
run multiple clusters

00:11:09.440 --> 00:11:12.410
in different
regions, while being

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:16.280
able to deploy a single
version of our API.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:21.260
And we are using features such
as horizontal pod autoscaling

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:24.470
to control the number
of API pods running,

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.950
according to the amounts
of the event per second

00:11:26.950 --> 00:11:29.950
that we are getting.

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:33.130
And also the GKE
node autoscaler,

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:36.880
which automatically increases or
decreases the number of nodes,

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.320
as in accordance with
the amount of the pods

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:44.350
that we are running
at the same time.

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:49.480
And on top of that, we are also
using a really nice feature

00:11:49.480 --> 00:11:52.510
called Cluster Federation.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:54.970
So Cluster Federation
is essentially

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:59.620
a Kubernetes feature, which
gives you an ability to build,

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:03.280
I would like to say,
like, a supercluster.

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:05.500
So you can have a
multiple clusters.

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:10.360
Every cluster can be in a
different region or even

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:13.930
a different cloud
provider or on premises,

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.300
but you can still manage
all of those clusters

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.852
from a single control plane.

00:12:19.852 --> 00:12:22.060
And it's a very powerful
feature, as you can imagine,

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:27.850
because I can have my
API running just anywhere

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:31.030
in the world, but still
controlling everything

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:34.540
from a single place.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.740
And finally, the other
very important piece here

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:41.200
is the Google HTTPS
load balancer.

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:45.520
So we are using something
that's called Global IP, which

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:52.130
allow us to have a single
anycast IP address for our API.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:57.250
And the request that is being
sent from the mobile device

00:12:57.250 --> 00:13:00.140
is resolving that IP address
to the nearest location

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:03.110
that Google has in
the same region,

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:06.920
and that's done
automatically for you.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.940
So initially, we had a couple of
clusters, one in North America

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:12.250
and the other one
in Western Europe.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.510
But eventually, we have
added more clusters

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:19.420
in the other places as
well, just to cover them.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:23.500
So to summarize that
point, we have a single IP.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:28.880
All of the events are being
sent to that single IP.

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:31.900
The IP is being resolved
to a local cluster

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:36.450
in the region that is
geographically close to the end

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.290
user player.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:47.370
And then the API processes the
event and pushes it forward.

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:49.490
So just to give
you a good example

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.610
of how having a local cluster
in certain geographies

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:55.190
improve the overall
latency, let's first

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:58.160
take a look on the median
latency for North America

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.150
or Western Europe.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:06.650
So it's 89 milliseconds and
54 milliseconds for Europe.

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:11.600
However, as you can see,
for players in Asia-Pacific,

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.610
without the local
clusters there,

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:17.570
the latency was on a
scale of 250 milliseconds,

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:24.650
almost five times longer than
for players in geographies

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:27.680
where we have local clusters.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:29.450
But thanks to the
Kubernetes Federation,

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:32.600
it was very easy for us to add
another cluster in Asia-Pacific

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.600
and that immediately
broke down the latency

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:41.950
to just 61 milliseconds, which
is almost 75% improvement.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:43.530
And we didn't have
to change anything

00:14:43.530 --> 00:14:45.890
to support this
additional cluster.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:49.390
The IP of our API
stayed exactly the same,

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:51.710
thanks to the Global IP feature.

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:55.010
We didn't have to update
the mobile application just

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:57.830
because we added a new cluster.

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:01.060
And once we had the
cluster up and running

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:03.780
under the Federation,
the Kubernetes

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:05.690
pushed the latest
version of our API

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:09.440
to that cluster and the
Google HTTP load balancer

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:13.280
started to route any requests
coming from Asia-Pacific

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:15.810
to that new local cluster.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:18.920
And as there will be more
players in other regions,

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:22.940
it will be as seamless for
us to add additional clusters

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:24.480
in the same way over time.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.390
Another piece of technology
we are using here

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:31.880
is a Google Cloud Endpoints.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:37.800
We obviously have to protect
the API from unauthorized use.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:40.460
And we are using Cloud Endpoints
to provide additional features

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:44.210
that we just didn't
want to develop ourself

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:46.686
and rather prefer
to rely on Google.

00:15:46.686 --> 00:15:48.560
So we are using Cloud
Endpoints to provide us

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:52.820
with the authentication,
rate control, and monitoring.

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:56.570
And let me put a little
bit more context here.

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:59.330
So of course,
authentication is important

00:15:59.330 --> 00:16:03.650
because we don't want
unauthorized devices or bots

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:06.320
to send us ingenuine data.

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:08.060
And by using Cloud
Endpoints, we ensure

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:11.930
that only devices providing the
correct authentication token

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:14.480
can send us the event data.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:18.230
Rate control is another very
important thing because even

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:22.160
while unauthenticated bots,
they cannot send the data

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:25.430
because they do
not have the token,

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.370
they can still make
attempts to send the data.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:30.950
And if there is
enough of them, it

00:16:30.950 --> 00:16:34.820
will be some sort of the
denial of service attack,

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.580
causing us to spend
extra money on more

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:41.750
resources such as
Kubernetes nodes or network

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.910
[INAUDIBLE] traffic.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:46.850
And finally, Cloud
Endpoints provides

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:50.250
many additional features,
such as monitoring our API.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:53.870
We can version the API.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:57.380
And maybe most importantly,
it can automatically

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:01.220
generate the mobile SDK
for iOS and Android.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:05.640
So once we introduce
a change to our API,

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.690
we can automatically
generate the SDK

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:11.750
and use that SDK in our
next version of the game.

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:20.420
Now that we are almost done
with the API part itself,

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:24.619
the final but very important
part is the Google Pub/Sub.

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:29.180
so in general, we are using
Pub/Sub to temporarily store

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:34.140
our event data that we are
getting from the devices.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:37.120
Pub/Sub is a
managed message bus,

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:40.430
which essentially means
that we can create a topic.

00:17:40.430 --> 00:17:42.610
Its a little bit
similar to, I would say,

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:48.300
water pipe, and then to send the
data from one side of that pipe

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:50.710
to the other side.

00:17:50.710 --> 00:17:53.370
And in our case, we
are using Pub/Sub

00:17:53.370 --> 00:17:56.550
because it's a good practice
to build systems in a way

00:17:56.550 --> 00:17:59.820
where the communication between
different parts of our system

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:02.490
will be asynchronous.

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:06.320
In that way, we can scale the
different parts separately,

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:08.260
one from the other.

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:13.440
And also, if some part is
under load or a pressure

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:17.500
or even down, we are
not losing any data.

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:21.900
So we are getting the event from
the API running on Kubernetes

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:25.020
and we are putting that
event into the Pub/Sub.

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:26.664
So let's see what happens next.

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:32.350
So after the ingestion
part is done,

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:36.380
we are getting into
the event processing.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:37.990
So what do I mean
by event processing?

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:43.570
Event processing its
actually many things

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:46.420
that we need or
have to do to allow

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.430
effective analysis of the data.

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:52.060
So first, we need
to parse the events

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:57.370
and then we need to make sure
they are complete and valid.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:01.640
Usually, after that, we will
want to enrich our data.

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:03.700
For example, one of the
most popular enrichments

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:07.130
are geolocation based on the
IP address of the request.

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:10.060
We would like to know the
city, country, perhaps

00:19:10.060 --> 00:19:13.670
some other data of the player.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:18.700
And after enrichment, we
are grouping and aggregating

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:21.130
the data because
sometimes we would

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:23.740
like to store different
events in different tables

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:24.870
in our database.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.860
I will get to that
a little bit later.

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:31.880
And we also want to calculate
some metrics on the fly.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.000
So for example, think
of the leaderboard.

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.010
So we want to
display a leaderboard

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:42.190
on our website to see how
many stars every player has.

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.830
So one way of doing that
would be to store everything

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:49.630
in some database and then make
some queries over the data

00:19:49.630 --> 00:19:53.560
and, let's say, refresh them
every minute or something.

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.660
What we are doing
here, we are actually

00:19:55.660 --> 00:20:00.010
calculating the leaderboard
on the fly at the time

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:03.360
that we process the events.

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:05.640
And that's pretty cool, right?

00:20:05.640 --> 00:20:08.880
So for event processing,
we are using Apache Beam.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:10.770
It's an open-source
project originated

00:20:10.770 --> 00:20:14.460
from Google several
years ago, and now it's

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.980
a part of Apache Foundation.

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:19.860
Or specifically, in our case,
we are using Google Cloud

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:23.760
Dataflow, which is a managed
version of the Apache Beam,

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:27.630
so we wouldn't need to worry
about deploying our own servers

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:31.560
or scaling them
or monitoring them

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:36.030
according to the volume of the
data that we are receiving.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.160
And Google Cloud
Dataflow is basically

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.500
a cloud-based
version of the ETL,

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:45.450
of the Extract
Transfer Load paradigm.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:49.760
And with Dataflow, we can read
the data from a certain source,

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:53.530
parse the data, transform
it, aggregate it, group it,

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:57.210
and, finally, send it
to another destination.

00:20:57.210 --> 00:21:03.330
And you can write the Google
Dataflow pipelines or jobs

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:05.760
in Java or Python.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:07.950
And it has a long
list of supported

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:12.380
connectors to various
Google Cloud products,

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:17.850
such as Pub/Sub or BigQuery or
Bigtable or Spanner or Cloud

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:21.550
SQL and so many others.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:24.720
And if you'd like, you
can even use Apache Beam

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:29.350
and run your jobs
on external data

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:34.240
processing engines
such a Spark or Flink,

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.970
which means it gives you
the freedom of running

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:42.800
your workloads just anywhere
without any kind of a vendor

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:43.780
lock-in.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:47.080
And this was something that
was very important for us

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:50.080
because we wanted
to give that freedom

00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:54.360
to be able to basically run
it everywhere if so we choose.

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:00.991
So in our case, we
are reading the data

00:22:00.991 --> 00:22:01.990
from the Google Pub/Sub.

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:04.480
We are parsing the data.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:07.490
We are doing the
aggregation and groupings,

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:11.260
and finally sending the
data to the Google BigQuery

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:15.942
and Bigtable for
further analysis.

00:22:15.942 --> 00:22:17.650
So the code that you
are seeing right now

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:23.200
is some kind of a pseudo
code because the actual code,

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:25.750
while it looks like very
similar, its a little bit

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:30.375
more verbose because Java is
a pretty verbose language.

00:22:30.375 --> 00:22:32.500
So I just couldn't fit it
to put that on the slide,

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:34.280
but you get the idea.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:38.740
And the Apache Beam SDK is
very powerful in the sense

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:42.100
that you can write a
very complicated code,

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:45.100
you can have a very
complicated pipeline,

00:22:45.100 --> 00:22:48.730
but your code is very short.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:51.340
And once you deploy your
code, the Google Dataflow

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:55.390
will automatically
build a graph,

00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:59.690
which you can see on the
right side of the slide.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.630
So that graph is being
automatically generated

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:04.110
from your code.

00:23:04.110 --> 00:23:08.960
You will see that on
the Google Cloud console

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.850
along some monitoring
information

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:14.010
on how your pipeline
is performing, meaning,

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:16.580
for example, how many
events you are processing,

00:23:16.580 --> 00:23:20.820
what is the delay of the
event or, in other words,

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:22.580
how much time does
it take to process

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:26.780
a single event from the second
it's being read from the Google

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:29.930
Pub/Sub until to the point that
it's being pushed to the Google

00:23:29.930 --> 00:23:31.200
BigQuery.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.050
And then you can
monitor those metrics

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:35.210
and build some sort
of alerts so you

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:38.930
can be notified if your
pipeline is performing

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:41.086
worse than you would expect.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:46.700
And here is the actual
graph, by the way,

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:51.000
for the demo that we
will be showing shortly.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.380
So in that graph,
you can see that we

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:56.830
are reading people data--

00:23:56.830 --> 00:23:59.590
actually, it's information
about our users--

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:02.980
and the event data, which
are the actual events

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:07.020
like login or moving from
one level to the other.

00:24:07.020 --> 00:24:10.900
And then we are doing the
parsing and aggregations

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:13.210
and essentially, finally,
we are pushing the data

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:15.050
to several destinations.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.380
In our case, it's
BigQuery and Bigtable,

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:22.630
and I will get to that
in just one minute.

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:27.470
And since we are using streaming
pipelines with Apache Beam,

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:31.340
the time window notion is
extremely important here.

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:35.680
If you are constantly reading
the data from some streaming

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:38.740
source, such as Google
Pub/Sub for example,

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:42.130
and you want to build
aggregations on the fly,

00:24:42.130 --> 00:24:47.350
time windows are essential
to be able to do that.

00:24:47.350 --> 00:24:49.210
So Google Dataflow
will automatically

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:52.120
assign every event to
the correct time window

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:54.910
based on the semantic
event time, which

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:58.600
means the true time when
the event actually happened,

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:00.840
as opposed to the
time that event

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:03.250
was received by the Dataflow.

00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:07.840
Now, if every part is in
the correct time window,

00:25:07.840 --> 00:25:10.180
it is very simple to
count, for example,

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:13.360
to count the amount of
stars each player has

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:18.390
to calculate the
leaderboard and to update

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:19.990
that in the real time.

00:25:19.990 --> 00:25:22.600
And there are many supported
window types in Google

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:25.750
Dataflow, for example,
fixed windows,

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:27.670
sliding windows,
session windows,

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:33.100
or even custom windows, if none
of the standard window types

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:35.380
doesn't work for you.

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:38.950
And windows are also
really helpful for taking

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:42.350
care of the late events.

00:25:42.350 --> 00:25:45.400
For example, you can imagine
that you are playing the game,

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:48.550
but you are in the
elevator or you

00:25:48.550 --> 00:25:52.960
are in the place where you have
a limited network coverage.

00:25:52.960 --> 00:25:55.450
And in that case, even
if your mobile device

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:57.670
generates the
events, those events

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:01.480
are not being sent because
you don't have the network.

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:05.470
And sometimes it can
take a lot of time

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:07.670
to actually send those events.

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:08.560
It can be minutes.

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:11.320
It can be even hours
sometimes, if you

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:14.524
are taking flight for example.

00:26:14.524 --> 00:26:18.580
And one of the greatest things
with the Google Dataflow

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:21.580
or Apache Beam in that
sense is that have

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.350
the notion of the late events.

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:28.510
So while the event
can really arrive late

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:30.940
by any amount of
time, it can still

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:35.260
be assigned to the
correct window,

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:39.490
to the correct time
window, so we can correctly

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:43.710
recalculate our leaderboards
at every given time.

00:26:48.110 --> 00:26:52.910
And there are many innovations
available with Apache Beam 2.x.

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:56.450
The latest version is 2.5.

00:26:56.450 --> 00:26:59.870
And those features, for example,
are dynamic destinations.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:03.260
They can help you to send
your data to various tables

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:08.750
based on the arbitrary
data that you are receiving

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:12.290
or, for example, automatic
schema detections making

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:16.450
your life so much easier
by not having to hard code

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:20.220
your schemas into the code, into
the pipeline code that you're

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:23.270
producing.

00:27:23.270 --> 00:27:25.880
And the other really
important part

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:28.100
here is the data mutability.

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:32.270
So the data mutability,
as it sounds,

00:27:32.270 --> 00:27:36.150
it means that some data
can change over time.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:38.930
And this may be a
little bit difficult

00:27:38.930 --> 00:27:43.390
with analytical databases, such
as Google BigQuery, which are

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:46.820
append only by their nature.

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:49.930
But what if you have
a table of the players

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:52.430
and you want to update
the player information,

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:54.590
for example, on the fly?

00:27:54.590 --> 00:27:57.950
Well, you may want
to update each player

00:27:57.950 --> 00:28:01.460
with its current status of the
amount of stars that they have

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:03.680
or their cash balance.

00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:06.680
And this is why we have
opted to use both Google

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:11.590
BigQuery for immutable data,
data that doesn't change

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:15.670
such as events, with Bigtable
for the mutable data,

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:17.870
such as player table.

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:20.570
And using Google
Cloud Dataflow, we

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:23.870
are sending those events
directly to the BigQuery,

00:28:23.870 --> 00:28:28.190
making its available for later
analysis by data scientists

00:28:28.190 --> 00:28:31.110
and our BI tools.

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:33.260
And for those people
who doesn't know,

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:36.950
Bigtable is a NoSQL
database powering

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:39.920
some really popular
applications, such as Gmail

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:42.260
or AdWords here at Google.

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:45.560
And it's ideal for storing
sparse information,

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:49.250
such as in our example,
people's profiles.

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:52.910
And it can support millions
of updates every second.

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:55.070
Bigtable is managed,
which means,

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:57.710
once again, we don't need to
be bothered with the management

00:28:57.710 --> 00:28:58.385
by ourself.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:07.630
And now that we are done
with the event processing,

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:11.850
let's talk a little bit
about the event analytics.

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:14.250
So for event analytics,
as you can imagine,

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:17.060
we are using Google BigQuery.

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:20.510
So this is a high level of
the BigQuery architecture.

00:29:20.510 --> 00:29:22.400
One of the greatest
things about the BigQuery

00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:26.740
is that it has storage and
compute scaled independently

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:28.720
on demand.

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:32.420
It helps us to analyze
billions of daily events

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:35.720
using standard SQL
at a great speed.

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:38.660
Most of our queries are
running only a few seconds,

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:41.930
despite the fact that they
scan terabytes of data.

00:29:41.930 --> 00:29:44.270
And since last year,
Google BigQuery

00:29:44.270 --> 00:29:47.660
allows you not only to
query native tables,

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:50.350
but also for the
federated tables.

00:29:50.350 --> 00:29:53.030
And federated tables,
it means that they

00:29:53.030 --> 00:29:56.660
can reside on other storage
systems such as Bigtable,

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:58.080
for example.

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:01.740
And since Bigtable itself
is a NoSQL database,

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:05.400
it means you cannot run
complex queries on it.

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:08.600
However, with Google BigQuery
and federated tables,

00:30:08.600 --> 00:30:12.560
you can have the power of
both Bigtable and BigQuery

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:15.920
by being able to run the
really complicated SQL

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:18.830
queries over NoSQL data.

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:25.670
And if you can still remember
the beginning of my talk--

00:30:25.670 --> 00:30:28.280
I barely can myself--

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:31.550
you may recall that we wanted
the event analytics platform

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:34.830
to be very cost effective.

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:38.210
And I also said that the
off-the-shelf solution

00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:42.800
that Jelly Button were
using had a significant cost

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:45.630
over the tens of thousands
of dollars every month.

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:48.440
So let's take a short
look on how much

00:30:48.440 --> 00:30:50.810
does it cost us today
to run the system

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:54.450
that I have just presented.

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:58.330
So we are using reOptimize.io
for Google Cloud Analytics.

00:30:58.330 --> 00:31:01.150
It's available from DoIT
International for free.

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:06.440
It's a pretty sophisticated tool
that have some cost analytics

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:08.120
and cost optimization platform.

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:10.670
And what you can see
here on the slide

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:13.190
is one of the cost
control dashboards.

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:16.070
And so you can see how
much we are spending

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:19.430
on Google Cloud every
month and some breakdown

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:23.160
by specific product over time.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:29.060
So in July, 2017, when we
launched the new system,

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:34.430
we were processing about
1.3 billion of daily events

00:31:34.430 --> 00:31:36.350
and the overall cost
of the Google Cloud

00:31:36.350 --> 00:31:41.250
for us was on the scale
of $1,500 per month.

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:42.830
That's all.

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:46.400
In January, 2018, we were
ingesting and processing

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:48.470
3.3 billion events--

00:31:48.470 --> 00:31:51.640
it's about three times more--

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:55.130
and our cost was just
about $2000 per month.

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:58.640
And finally, during May,
2018, just a few months ago,

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:01.180
we were already
processing 6 million--

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:04.960
6 billion, I'm sorry,
of daily events.

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:08.110
And still is our cost a
little bit less than--

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:12.710
well, actually, a little
bit more than $3,000.

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:18.850
So if you compare that
to the $45,000 to $65,000

00:32:18.850 --> 00:32:21.970
that we were paying
every month before that,

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:25.000
that's a pretty significant
cost saving, right?

00:32:28.700 --> 00:32:30.222
You can clap.

00:32:30.222 --> 00:32:32.026
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:36.720
Thank you, guys.

00:32:36.720 --> 00:32:39.210
So it took us about five
weeks of three people

00:32:39.210 --> 00:32:40.530
to build the new system.

00:32:40.530 --> 00:32:42.540
We have spent about
two weeks exploring

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:45.180
different architectures
and prototyping

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.900
before we could settle on
the current state of affairs.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:53.560
Then we took another week to
make our code production-ready,

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:56.250
another week of testing
everything in parallel

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:59.080
to running the previous system.

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:02.100
And finally, over the
course of the fifth week,

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:06.720
we have gradually moved all
of our event analytics traffic

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:08.910
to the new system.

00:33:08.910 --> 00:33:12.330
And right now I
would like to call

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:15.090
Yoram, a customer
reliability engineer at DoIT

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:17.374
International for the demo.

00:33:17.374 --> 00:33:19.778
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:20.022 --> 00:33:21.730
YORAM BEN-YAACOV:
Thank you [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:30.800
Thank you, Vadim.

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:32.430
Hi, everybody.

00:33:32.430 --> 00:33:36.900
So we're all familiar with
the common flow in which data

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:38.130
is coming to Pub/Sub.

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:40.410
And from there, we
take it using Dataflow,

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:42.170
doing whatever we
want to do with it,

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:46.640
and putting it into BigQuery.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:48.680
Since this is the
[INAUDIBLE] I'm

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:52.850
going to show a little bit about
what we are doing with Bigtable

00:33:52.850 --> 00:33:56.870
and why is it more dynamic
and more interesting.

00:33:56.870 --> 00:33:58.730
So as Vadim said,
last year Google

00:33:58.730 --> 00:34:01.130
announced the federated
queries, which

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:05.780
allows you to run SQL queries
on unstructured data that

00:34:05.780 --> 00:34:07.880
reside on Bigtable.

00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:16.580
This allows us to run the same
SQL queries on virtual tables

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:20.870
on BigQuery that actually
belongs to Bigtable.

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:24.340
So what we got
was two requests--

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:26.900
were two requests in
the latest version.

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:31.670
The first one was to create
a dynamic leaderboard

00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:33.980
for bigger amount
of users, something

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:38.600
that will not be
calculated in Dataflow,

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:41.270
but will be taken outside.

00:34:41.270 --> 00:34:45.679
And the second thing
was to be able to remove

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:49.540
a user from the system
according to the GDPR compliance

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:51.810
with the GDPR guidelines.

00:34:51.810 --> 00:34:55.730
To solve this, we decided
to actually put Bigtable

00:34:55.730 --> 00:35:01.380
into the flow and to split the
data according to the need.

00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:04.460
So we have three types of data.

00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:08.360
We have the users,
which contains the PII.

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:12.020
We have the events, which
doesn't contain any PII,

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:15.410
and the leaderboard, again,
doesn't contain any PII.

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:22.050
So this is as part,
very small part

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:25.380
of the pipeline in Dataflow.

00:35:25.380 --> 00:35:28.800
I just want you to see a little
bit about what we are doing.

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:30.840
We actually have two main flows.

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:37.110
We have the one on the left
that actually takes data

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:43.720
from people, messages that are
coming to the people in topic

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.660
on Pub/Sub.

00:35:45.660 --> 00:35:49.080
It's doing all kind
of fancy enrichments.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:53.140
It depends, again, on the
game and the requirements.

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:56.470
And from there
directly to Bigtable.

00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:05.180
Let's see a little bit about
what we have on Bigtable.

00:36:09.970 --> 00:36:12.650
So actually, on Bigtable,
we have two tables.

00:36:12.650 --> 00:36:16.100
We have the leaderboards
and we have the people.

00:36:16.100 --> 00:36:18.770
Now, if I want to
see one of the users,

00:36:18.770 --> 00:36:21.500
for example, on Bigtable,
then I will probably

00:36:21.500 --> 00:36:23.240
do something like
this and I will get

00:36:23.240 --> 00:36:25.430
all the history of the user.

00:36:25.430 --> 00:36:28.040
To the people who don't
know, Bigtable actually

00:36:28.040 --> 00:36:31.590
gives us kind of
three-dimensional table.

00:36:31.590 --> 00:36:34.620
So we have not only the
columns and the family names,

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:37.640
we also have the time
period, a time series

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:40.640
of data per each row.

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:45.770
But this is not something
which is that easy to utilize.

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:50.270
So what we did was actually
to define the same table using

00:36:50.270 --> 00:36:54.980
this JSON, to take this JSON,
and to put it into Bigtable

00:36:54.980 --> 00:37:00.310
and defined a federated extended
table, the BigQuery table.

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:04.630
Sorry, the Bigtable
table on BigQuery.

00:37:04.630 --> 00:37:06.580
I have too many bigs
and too many tables.

00:37:06.580 --> 00:37:09.530
My apologies.

00:37:09.530 --> 00:37:10.030
Sorry.

00:37:10.030 --> 00:37:11.510
You have to do
something about it.

00:37:11.510 --> 00:37:15.230
So actually, this is the
schema of the people table

00:37:15.230 --> 00:37:20.890
that reside on Bigtable as
it represent on BigQuery.

00:37:20.890 --> 00:37:24.940
Now, from here, if I
want to do the same query

00:37:24.940 --> 00:37:28.095
on the same user,
it's relatively easy.

00:37:30.860 --> 00:37:33.740
And of course, I
can see in one line

00:37:33.740 --> 00:37:37.810
who is the user, what
is his age, gender.

00:37:37.810 --> 00:37:39.740
Again, I defined
over here that I

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:45.320
want to see only the latest
version of each of the columns.

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:47.480
Since it's a sparse
data, I will get

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:49.190
only the relevant
data that exist,

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:51.280
the latest version over here.

00:37:59.390 --> 00:38:02.660
So I want to go back to
the second flow and--

00:38:05.694 --> 00:38:08.110
now, the second flow is actually
the more interesting one,

00:38:08.110 --> 00:38:12.070
the way I see it, because
the second flow takes

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:15.220
all the messages that
are event-related.

00:38:15.220 --> 00:38:18.180
So it lists them to a
different topic on Pub/Sub.

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:21.790
It takes all the data, all
the messages that arrive,

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:25.930
and it will do four things
with these messages.

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:31.640
The first one, store the
row message into BigQuery.

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:36.020
And I did make a mistake over
here, directly to BigQuery.

00:38:36.020 --> 00:38:38.780
This is actually the
single source of truth.

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:42.050
The customer can do whatever
analytics on the BigQuery

00:38:42.050 --> 00:38:44.720
using the BigQuery engine.

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:47.120
It will run fast
and it will have

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:50.890
all the advantages of BigQuery.

00:38:50.890 --> 00:38:55.810
The second part of the flow will
take the event, the same event.

00:38:55.810 --> 00:39:00.450
Now we will do some kind,
again, of any fancy enrichment

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:05.740
and it will update or
actually insert new records

00:39:05.740 --> 00:39:07.400
into two tables.

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:10.630
The first one is to
the people table.

00:39:10.630 --> 00:39:16.090
This way, I don't have to
calculate outside of Bigtable

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:21.640
the status of each user once
I entered new row on new value

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:29.690
for each of these,
the stars and cash.

00:39:29.690 --> 00:39:34.090
The user will automatically will
have only the latest version

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:35.770
that we entered.

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:39.190
The second thing
is the leaderboard.

00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:41.370
Now, when I'm doing
this I actually

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:45.340
don't need Bigtable to
handle all the windowing

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:50.890
and splitting and keeping
the late arrivals.

00:39:50.890 --> 00:39:55.210
Not only that, when I'm doing
windowing on Dataflow that I

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:58.800
need to define a
windows, time periods,

00:39:58.800 --> 00:40:03.640
early firing over here, the
data is up to date all the time.

00:40:03.640 --> 00:40:06.490
Again, we are utilizing
the very fast response,

00:40:06.490 --> 00:40:09.250
the millisecond of some
millisecond response

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:11.120
of Bigtable.

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:15.520
So let's look a bit
into the tables.

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:25.670
So what we did was to
take the leaderboard--

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:28.920
I want to start, first of all,
with the more trivial one,

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:33.910
which is the immutable events.

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:40.330
Again, this is a very standard
BigQuery table, native BigQuery

00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:43.330
table, I'm sure that
you're all familiar with.

00:40:43.330 --> 00:40:48.070
So I will continue to the next
one, which is the leaderboard.

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:50.600
This is the one that
is more interesting.

00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:56.860
This is the table that exists
physically on Bigtable,

00:40:56.860 --> 00:41:01.120
but we represented
it using this JSON.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:03.100
This is very small
JSON of I want

00:41:03.100 --> 00:41:08.110
to know only the star balance
and the cash balance per user.

00:41:08.110 --> 00:41:10.240
The key that we used
over here was actually

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:13.540
the date and the user ID.

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:18.700
Now, if I want to query
for a specific date,

00:41:18.700 --> 00:41:23.830
I want to see-- let's say I
want to see who are the five

00:41:23.830 --> 00:41:26.620
leaders for the--
it's not for the 24th

00:41:26.620 --> 00:41:29.125
because I just changed the
query today to the 25th.

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:38.600
So as you can see,
I have the key.

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:43.900
From the key I got the user
ID and I know exactly how much

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:45.950
star balance and cash balance.

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:50.930
In this case, I ordered it
according to the star balance

00:41:50.930 --> 00:41:54.200
because usually this
is what we care.

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:58.580
Now, if somebody from marketing,
for example, or from sales

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:03.080
want to better understand
who are the best spenders

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:05.930
or leaders for a
period of time, I

00:42:05.930 --> 00:42:11.540
can always go and also do the
row between, and then give

00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:14.050
a date range or a key range.

00:42:14.050 --> 00:42:17.510
And according to this key
range, get all the relevant data

00:42:17.510 --> 00:42:20.180
that reside inside this range.

00:42:20.180 --> 00:42:24.950
In this case, I did from
the 22nd till today.

00:42:24.950 --> 00:42:28.010
And I got a different
list of people.

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:31.350
This time they are sorted
by the cash balance.

00:42:31.350 --> 00:42:35.390
Now again, think about the fact
that all this data actually

00:42:35.390 --> 00:42:36.260
resides in Bigtable.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:41.910
This is something that
was not possible using

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:47.350
CBT or any other common
tools working with Bigtable.

00:42:47.350 --> 00:42:53.970
But once we are working with
BigQuery and federated tables,

00:42:53.970 --> 00:42:57.420
I can do this
without any problem.

00:42:57.420 --> 00:43:01.500
Now, the last thing
that we still have

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:04.440
is how we remove the user.

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:10.370
So again, doing it with BigQuery
was a bit more complicated.

00:43:10.370 --> 00:43:16.590
Here, if I have this user,
all I have to do is delete row

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:17.930
and that's it.

00:43:17.930 --> 00:43:28.720
And hopefully, the gods of
demo will not do anything.

00:43:28.720 --> 00:43:30.370
And yes, he's out.

00:43:33.340 --> 00:43:35.910
One last thing before I finish.

00:43:35.910 --> 00:43:41.470
Vadim spoke about part
of the flow that we did.

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:44.350
This is to date the [INAUDIBLE],,
which is one of the operations

00:43:44.350 --> 00:43:47.020
that we are speaking about,
that we presented earlier.

00:43:47.020 --> 00:43:52.290
So we can play with it and get
80% to 90% of the flow already.

00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:53.400
That's it.

00:43:53.400 --> 00:43:54.528
Thank you.

00:43:54.528 --> 00:43:57.018
[APPLAUSE]

